Ported (Python to Rust) into crates/kgraf-session/src/reduction.rs:
the `LastNObservations` history processor's core algorithm (decision 0057,
ROADMAP Phase 3 "Compaction"): keep the most recent N observations unmasked,
replace every older one with a placeholder, and never touch a pinned entry
(upstream never touches the first entry specifically, the instance template;
kGraf generalizes "never touch" to an explicit pinned-index set the caller
supplies plus a self-reported `Reducible::is_pinned`, per 0057: "the original
instruction and pinned items are never masked"). kGraf's port generalizes
"observation" from SWE-agent's message-type tag to the `Reducible` trait, so
the same algorithm works over any transcript entry type; the "keep last N"
default (5, the paper-cited window this file itself names) is unchanged.

Not a literal match, corrected here after an owner review found the earlier
version of this file overclaimed it:

- Upstream's placeholder is dynamic -- `"Old environment output: (N lines
  omitted)"`, naming how much was cut. kGraf's placeholder
  (`MaskConfig::placeholder`) is a fixed string the caller supplies; it does
  not compute or report a size. If that detail matters later, it is a small
  addition to `reduce`'s masking loop, not implemented here.
- kGraf never applies a placeholder that would not shrink the entry (an
  observation shorter than the placeholder is left alone): SWE-agent's
  `LastNObservations` has no equivalent check, since its placeholder always
  replaces content upstream considers already-consumed context regardless of
  size.
- `always_remove_output_for_tags` / `always_keep_output_for_tags` are not
  ported: kGraf's pinning is the explicit index set and trait method
  above, not a tag convention.
- The truncation step (masking's counterpart when it alone does not fit the
  budget) is not from this file at all -- see `third_party/openhands/UPSTREAM`
  -- and, unlike either source, groups entries sharing a
  `Reducible::truncation_unit` (a tool call and its result) so truncation
  never drops one half of a pair.
